INT. CHEESE AGING CAVE - NIGHT

A limestone tunnel hung with lanterns. Wooden shelves stacked with wheels of cheese, each stamped with a date. The air is cold, damp, and sharp with ammonia. Somewhere, water drips.

INSPECTOR HONORIA WYNDHAM-LEE (50s, tailored overcoat, gloves) walks the aisle with a leather satchel, tapping the wheels with a small silver hammer.

Behind her trails FERMIN OKONKWO-DAHL (30s, cheesemonger, rubber apron), who holds a lantern high. Further back, the owner, MADAME SOLANGE ARQUETTE (60s, shawl, a wedge of cheese in her fist), muttering as she chews.

A fourth figure, ROLLO PINKERTON (40s, apprentice, nervous grin), drifts at the end of the line, tidying shelves nobody asked him to tidy.

HONORIA
(to the group)
I'll be quick. Where was Monsieur Brisard found?

FERMIN
Between those two racks. Behind the Gruyère.

HONORIA
Face up?

FERMIN
Face down.

HONORIA
In the rind?

FERMIN
In the rind.

SOLANGE
(to herself)
Forty years I have made this cheese.

HONORIA
Madame Arquette?

SOLANGE
Forty years and never a murder.

HONORIA
We aren't calling it that yet.

SOLANGE
You didn't say "accident."

Honoria crouches by the rack. She runs a glove along the wooden slats and holds it to the light: a streak of blue-green mold.

HONORIA
Rollo.

ROLLO
Yes, Inspector?

HONORIA
When did you last wash these shelves?

ROLLO
Tuesday.

HONORIA
This mold is a week old.

ROLLO
I might have missed a spot.

HONORIA
Rollo, did you see Monsieur Brisard on Tuesday?

ROLLO
(too fast)
No.

FERMIN
You did. You gave him his lunch.

ROLLO
That was Monday.

SOLANGE
(muttering)
Monday, Tuesday. All the same in a cave.

HONORIA
(to the room)
Everyone stay where you are. Fermin, the lantern lower. There.

The light drops. On the stone floor, almost invisible, a trail of fine white powder leads away down the tunnel.

HONORIA (CONT'D)
(quietly)
Now that is interesting.
